WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.636 --> 00:00:21.540
By height and width,

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.600
this is the slimmest
building in the world.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.760
What does it take from an
engineering perspective

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.550
to actually construct something like this?

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:31.050
It's a combination of very
advanced wind tunnel testing

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.670
that wasn't available a few years ago.

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:34.240
Okay.
Much stronger concrete

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.628
than was available a few years ago.

00:00:36.628 --> 00:00:39.033
And a number of other engineering factors.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:45.400
It really turned out to be
this amazing juxtaposition

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:46.270
of old and new.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:48.270
As we stand here and you look up

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.340
you have this amazing
restored copper lantern

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.970
that we refabricated off original drawings

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:55.380
and then this amazing
contemporary tower behind it.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.570
So it's that old and new play

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.670
that we were very excited
about as developers.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.920
You don't get a chance
to do this very often.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.320
I'm on the 43rd floor in a
$28.75 million apartment.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:20.610
This is 50 feet of glass on Central Park.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.380
The apartment is obviously quite nice

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:25.480
but the real star of the show is the view.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:32.720
In the kitchen, when you're
taking a break from cooking

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.040
or watching someone cook for you

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.930
you can look out to Columbus
Circle, to New Jersey

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:41.893
all the way down Central Park to Harlem.

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:46.420
Each apartment takes up an entire floor

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:49.070
so when you step off the
elevator, you're home.

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:51.660
The front of the apartment
is entertaining spaces,

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.290
the back is bedrooms.

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:54.950
This apartment has three bedrooms,

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:56.560
so as you walk down a hallway

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.040
that's filled with more closet space

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.370
than most New Yorkers have as living space

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:01.960
you go into one of the three bedrooms

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:04.570
where there is a marble covered bathroom.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:07.030
And then the bedroom itself.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:09.150
You have views of the
Empire State Building.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:10.690
You can actually see the terra cotta

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.250
on the side of the facade.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:13.880
And of course you have views

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:15.633
through all of southern Manhattan.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:18.890
I'm here in the master bathroom.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:21.630
Next to me is a bathtub
that was custom designed

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:22.730
for this space.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:25.720
The walls and floors and sinks,

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:28.160
even the insides of the sinks, are onyx.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:29.420
The walls are more polished,

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:31.480
the floors are more
matte, if that matters.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:34.430
And then over here is the shower.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:38.490
And there is a window looking
straight out across Manhattan.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:41.583
So even in the bathroom you've
got a view in this apartment.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:53.450
I'm on the 82nd floor, above
all the residential spaces

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:57.310
standing in front of 800 tons of steel

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:59.350
that comprise the tuned mass damper

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.730
that basically counteracts
the sway of the building.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:05.360
Now, even if this wasn't here,
the building would be fine.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.310
It's exclusively for comfort.

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:10.700
And what it does is when the
building sways in one direction

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:12.950
it moves in the opposite direction

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:15.780
to keep the building as
straight and steady as possible.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:19.880
Now, it's not just the steel,
it's also filled with sand.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:23.490
So this is a colossal
contraption that takes up,

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:27.653
as you can see, the entire
floor of the building.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.690
So we're on the exterior hoist,

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:33.040
I wouldn't want to say plummeting

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.160
but it certainly feels
like we're plummeting.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.293
We're descending on the
exterior of the building.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.550
It's definitely not for the faint of heart

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:44.230
or for anyone who has ever
confronted their own mortality

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:46.020
because there's a lot of jitters

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.704
and it's something that the
people who are building this

00:03:48.704 --> 00:03:51.000
are quite inured to.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.476
But for a casual visitor
it is, I would say,

00:03:54.476 --> 00:03:56.693
absolutely terrifying.

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:04.410
We're standing in the 94 year old

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.800
historic Steinway Hall Atrium

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.950
which, as you can see,
is a pretty crazy place.

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:13.260
It's designed to give a
sense of place and grandeur

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:15.470
and ceremony to its original purpose

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:17.470
namely selling pianos.

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:22.430
And as a result, you see
intricately carved marble columns.

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:24.640
You see this plaster coffered ceiling.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:26.470
You see this very large mural

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:28.370
that's in the process of being restored.

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:29.630
And you see it culminating

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:32.350
into this colossal crystal chandelier.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:34.610
Which is hanging above the only thing

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:36.660
that doesn't quite make sense

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:41.660
namely the model of the 91
story 111 West 57th Street Tower

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.573
which is adjacent to
the Steinway building.

